# Environment for local signed builds. Copy to .env and fill in.
#
# `make package` loads this file. A bare `npx tauri build` does NOT — the
# Tauri CLI has no dotenv support — so either go through make, or source it
# into your own shell first:
#
#   set -a; . ./.env; set +a
#
# These are the variable names the Tauri CLI reads, which are NOT always the
# names the GitHub secrets use. The mapping lives in
# .github/workflows/release.yml; docs/development.md has the table.
#
# DO NOT commit the .env file.

# --- Updater signing ----------------------------------------------------
#
# Required for any bundle. To skip signing on a local test build, use
# `npx tauri build --no-sign --bundles app` instead of setting these.
#
# If this key is lost, already-shipped apps can never update again.
#
# Give the key inline, or point at the file, not both:
TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY="dW50cnVzdGVkIGNvbW1lbnQ6..."
# TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH="/path/to/netscope.key"
#
# Must be SET even when the key has no password. Setting it empty is not the
# same as leaving it unset: unset makes the CLI stop on an interactive
# password prompt.
TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSWORD=""

# --- macOS code signing -------------------------------------------------
#
# Two ways to give the CLI a certificate. It reads both itself — local
# signing is not a different code path from CI.
#
# 1. From your login keychain (preferred locally). Import the .p12 once by
#    double-clicking it in Finder, check it with
#      security find-identity -v -p codesigning
#    and set the identity to a substring matching exactly one entry.
#    "Developer ID Application" is normally enough and is what CI uses.
APPLE_SIGNING_IDENTITY="Developer ID Application"
#
# 2. From a base64 blob, the way CI does it — it has no login keychain, so
#    the CLI builds a temporary one. Useful locally only for reproducing a
#    CI signing failure; it puts the .p12 password in a file on disk.
#      base64 -i certificate.p12 | pbcopy
# APPLE_CERTIFICATE="..."
# APPLE_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD="the password you set when exporting"

# --- macOS notarization -------------------------------------------------
#
# APPLE_PASSWORD is an app-specific password, not your Apple ID password.
# Create one at https://appleid.apple.com.
#
# Mind the name: the GitHub secret is called APPLE_APP_SPECIFIC_PASSWORD, but
# the variable the CLI reads is APPLE_PASSWORD. Setting the former locally
# does nothing.
APPLE_ID="your-apple-id@example.com"
APPLE_PASSWORD="xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx"
APPLE_TEAM_ID="XXXXXXXXXX"

# --- Publishing ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# Only needed to publish a release from your machine; CI uses GITHUB_TOKEN.
# Create at https://github.com/settings/tokens with "repo" scope, or a
# fine-grained token with "contents: write".
GH_TOKEN="ghp_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
